---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const title = 'FAQ | defamationcalc.com';
const description = 'Frequently asked questions about defamation, libel, and slander: elements, defenses, actual malice, and statute of limitations.';
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={description}>
  <h1>Frequently Asked Questions</h1>
  <h2>What are the elements of a defamation claim?</h2>
  <p>Generally: (1) a false statement of fact (not opinion); (2) publication to a third party; (3) fault by the defendant (negligence for private figures; actual malice for public figures); and (4) damages (or per se defamation for presumed damages).</p>
  <h2>What is the difference between libel and slander?</h2>
  <p>Libel is defamation in written, printed, or permanently recorded form (including online posts and videos). Slander is defamation in spoken or transitory form. Libel is generally treated as more serious because it is permanent and can reach a wider audience.</p>
  <h2>What is defamation per se?</h2>
  <p>Statements so inherently harmful that damages are presumed without proof: (1) false accusation of a serious crime; (2) false statement that a person has a loathsome disease; (3) false statement about a person&rsquo;s professional conduct; (4) false statement about sexual misconduct. In per se cases, plaintiffs can recover without proving specific economic loss.</p>
  <h2>What is actual malice?</h2>
  <p>The standard from <em>New York Times Co. v. Sullivan</em> (1964): the defendant published the false statement knowing it was false, or with reckless disregard for whether it was false or true. Recklessness means the defendant had serious doubts about the truth but published anyway. This is a subjective standard &mdash; it focuses on the defendant&rsquo;s state of mind, not reasonableness.</p>
  <h2>Is opinion protected from defamation claims?</h2>
  <p>Expressions of pure opinion (&ldquo;I think he&rsquo;s dishonest&rdquo;) are generally protected under the First Amendment. But statements of opinion that imply false underlying facts (&ldquo;In my opinion, he committed fraud&rdquo;) can be actionable if they imply a false factual basis.</p>
  <h2>What is the statute of limitations for defamation?</h2>
  <p>Most states: 1&ndash;3 years from publication. The single-publication rule (majority approach) starts the clock at first publication, not each re-post. Some states follow the discovery rule (clock starts when plaintiff knew or should have known).</p>
</BaseLayout>
